5	// Regression coverage for form defaults that turn a native Error's "message"
6	// slot into a structured value. A caller may hand in a raw Error whose
7	// "message" is not an own property at reception (new Error() carries the
8	// empty-string message on the prototype), and a successful form can then
9	// either create a message object (a default at failure.message.label, with
10	// further defaults nested under it) or write a number/boolean/string default
11	// directly at failure.message. The live run input kept that value correctly,
12	// but the independent copies handed to business attempts and to compensation
13	// are rebuilt from a structured clone — and structuredClone serializes an
14	// Error's message slot as a STRING: an object became "[object Object]", a
15	// number "5" and a boolean "false", so the already-validated fields vanished
16	// from the action's input. These tests pin the repair: the form-written
17	// message reaches every independent copy with its original type, structure
18	// and value, multi-level parents come whole, the value stays an Error, and
19	// every copy remains independent of the run input, the caller and the other
20	// copies.
